  "created": "2026-09-15T12:48:41.123Z",
  "description": "Pair two machines that cannot see each other with a short code, once, then send and receive for as long as they are switched on. The link re-establishes itself after a network change, a relay outage, or either machine rebooting, and the identity that makes that possible is stored encrypted (DPAPI on Windows, 0600 inside a 0700 directory on Unix).",
  "id": "Tailcat.Link",
  "isPrerelease": false,
  "lastEdited": "2026-09-15T12:51:52.96Z",
  "licenseFile": "LICENSE",
  "licenseUrl": "https://aka.ms/deprecateLicenseUrl",
  "listed": true,
  "packageHash": "KG7nlCtJMSRuRneKEuqUdJ63DAb6WWHJueSkbVUhpTjEegJOu3uVoe0zC0hh71ZhlsRMlh6l/CQ+Xb48zx0f4A==",
  "packageHashAlgorithm": "SHA512",
  "packageSize": 355207,
  "projectUrl": "https://github.com/b-y-t-e/tailcat-link",
  "published": "2026-09-15T12:48:41.123Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "One exchange: RequestAsync, NotifyAsync and SendAsync carry content of any size — from a kilobyte of JSON to a 20 GB file — in blocks that neither machine holds more than a few of, resume in both directions when a session dies, and run the handler once. The library sets no size limit. A machine without exchanges, which today is only the browser client, is sent single frames and cannot take transfers. Hole punching between two different NATs is verified: a home connection to an LTE carrier NAT, 69 ms relayed down to 30 ms direct. Where QUIC is missing, including Windows 10, the session is carried by the relay instead and works.",
